  "hints": [
    "Consider a substring interval while selecting a subsequence inside it.",
    "Matching endpoints can surround an optimal inner palindrome; otherwise skip either endpoint.",
    "Process left endpoints backward. Before updating dp[right], save its old value; matching ends use old diagonal+2, otherwise max(below,left).",
    "A one-dimensional table preserves the interval recurrence in O(n) space. Save the previous diagonal before overwriting it, and initialize every single character to length one."
  ],
  "editorial": "For equal endpoints, an optimal inner subsequence can be surrounded by the two equal characters. Otherwise at least one endpoint is absent, so take the larger result after dropping either end. Bottom-up evaluation implements this recursive interval relation without a length-1000 recursion stack. One row plus the old diagonal is sufficient. The independent oracle enumerates every index subset and checks its literal selected string for palindrome equality.",
